/*!

*\page 03_coding_format Coding style
*
*The script program is easy and simple.
*- The script program is not case-sensitive: the system converts the script code to lowercase as soon as you run it. The only text that is not converted to lowercase is text inside quotation marks.
*- All variables are global. But variables inside a function is local. You cannot reach variables inside a function. Functions cannot reach global variables either. You need to send it as an argument.
*- GPIO definitions are global.
*- No need to specify a variable type. String, Int or float are the three supported types. When the variable initializes, the system determines if the variable is a string, int or float. Reassigning variable changes the variables nature to the new type<br>
*  <br>If you use the same variable name with another type initializer, the system converts the type according to the new type, whether you change a variable from text to a number or vice versa.<br>
*  Maximum allowed variables are 32 (defined in tokens.h). Space has no effect between words. <br>New line, semicolon, carriage return or NULL terminator determines the end of an instruction line. 
*  You can write several instructions inside one line separated by semicolon.
*  For example:  <br>
*			put PA_4, 0; &nbsp; &nbsp;delay 4,msec; &nbsp;&nbsp; put PA_4,1;<br>
*  A space separates an instruction from its arguments. For example: arg1,arg2. No space is required between arguments.
*  A comma separates different operands or arguments. Commas should also be used in a for loop, not semicolons.
*/
